title: First T-shirts
---
body:

Knitting is definitely a slippery slope into all sort of other crafts! I'd been vaguely wanting to sew my own clothes for years, and since I started knitting it didn't seem so intimidating to learn. Unsurprisingly, many of my knitting friends also sew and could help me get started.

A couple friends from my craft club got together for a T-shirt making day. Meghan was our mentor since she's been sewing for 5 years and has made dozens of pieces of clothing for herself. Karen had some experience sewing, and I hadn't done it since a class in high school.

At Meghan suggestion, I made the Jackson Tee by Helen's Closet. While the pattern was very easy, sewing with stretchy knits was harder than working with wovens would have been. On the other hand, most woven shirt patterns are more complicated. She also advised us on buying fabric, and I picked out a cream interlock knit, which was a good weight for a T-shirt. 

Meghan has a projector for patterns, which is very fancy but a bit tricky to work with since my hand kept blocking the light when I traced the pattern onto the fabric. It took me a while to remember how to use a sewing machine, but with some patience and encouragement from Meghan I was off to the races. It took longer than we had planned for, but at the end of the day I had a homemade T-shirt that fit!

I enjoyed it so much, I went home and made a second one with the other half of the fabric and a paper pattern, and then four more after that.

Details:
* View B, long length
* Size 20 for the torso, size 22 for armsyce and sleeves
* Fabric is 100% cotton knit


